**A measured instance of the coverage gap, on the change that fixes a coverage gap** (voicebox-beads-l79v, `17fcd2b`): the fast gate reported PASS with `coverage UNKNOWN ... 3 changed file(s) NOT demonstrated to be exercised`, and the truth was recoverable only by grepping the log by hand:

- the gate's command is `node --test $(node scripts/test-lanes.mjs --lane unit)`;
- my changed test file DID run — its three new test names are in the log — and all 11 assertions passed;
- the log names **no file at all** (no per-file boundary, no `tests/<file>`), so the reporter cannot attribute a test to a file, and the artifact says UNKNOWN for a change whose coverage was in fact complete.

This is the "nothing says so" case from the other direction: not 1-of-89-hidden, but 11-of-11-invisible. **It also shows the circularity worth writing into the field:** the unit lane's file list is produced BY `scripts/test-lanes.mjs`, so for a change to the classifier the gate's selection and the thing under test are the same artifact — a classifier change that demoted files would run fewer tests, go green, and leave no trace in the log. That is why l79v ships a pinned per-lane counts test rather than trusting the run. For the reader line, this is a second argument that dispatching is not enough on its own: the artifact needs the *names* it ran, not only the counts.

READ-ONLY SCOPING RESULT. No edits. The three existing rules explain the whole picture:

  public/style.css:413   .list-tools input::placeholder { color: var(--muted); opacity: 1; }
  public/style.css:1113  .composer input::placeholder { color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--muted) 62%, transparent); }
  public/pip-mic.mjs:293 input::placeholder { color: var(--muted); }

So the split is already real in the CSS: .list-tools is scoped and correct - that is the element I measured passing at 6.12:1 light / 6.56:1 dark, and it uses the page's own token, which is why it adapts to the theme. .composer is scoped but deliberately translucent at 62%, which is exactly the alpha I measured on #utterance (2.68:1 light, 3.46:1 dark) - that is voicebox-beads-8wm8, correctly split out. Everything else on the page has NO ::placeholder rule at all, which is why 21 of 22 on / and 1 of 1 on Help fall back to the user agent grey.

WHY THE OBVIOUS FIX IS NOT JUST 'ADD A GLOBAL RULE': specificity, not intent, decides who wins. '.list-tools input::placeholder' and '.composer input::placeholder' both outrank a bare 'input::placeholder', so a page-level rule would cover the 22 uncovered elements while leaving both deliberate rules in place. That is the shape I would propose - one rule using the page's own --muted, so it inherits the theme the way file-filter already does. BUT I am not asserting that precedence from the spec: it is exactly the kind of claim I have got wrong twice today by reasoning instead of measuring, so the first thing the change would do is measure the computed colour of the .composer and .list-tools elements BEFORE and AFTER adding it, and fail if either moved.

FOR 8wm8 SEPARATELY: the composer needs 62% -> something that clears 4.5:1 while staying visibly de-emphasised, in BOTH themes (2.68 -> >=4.5 light, 3.46 -> >=4.5 dark). The options are a higher mix percentage or a dedicated token; the percentage can be derived rather than guessed, and I will compute it rather than eyeball it.

Also worth noting for whoever lands this: the dark theme here is driven by <html data-theme>, NOT prefers-color-scheme, and the pages expose id=theme-toggle in the room. Any test for this MUST drive data-theme or it will assert the light theme twice and pass while dark is broken - which is the mistake I made in my first measurement pass.

PLAN ADDENDUM — two requirements from coord, added before approval rather than discovered during the work.

## 1. Computed-style and focus/contrast become browser proof, not CSS-text comparison
The plan already asserted computed values; this makes the accessibility half explicit, because the craft floor's `Contrast` line is a requirement rather than a style preference (*"body and placeholder text ≥4.5:1, large text ≥3:1"*). Measured in the browser, from the **resolved** `light-dark()` values in each scheme, never from the source text:
- body text against body background, and the placeholder of `#asset-name` against the input background, both ≥ **4.5:1**, in light and dark;
- `--muted` secondary text against `--ground`, since that is the pair a palette move would most easily break;
- the `:focus-visible` outline — 2px solid `--accent`, 2px offset — present and drawing on a really focused element, in both schemes, since coord's bead names focus among the things to preserve;
- the input's computed `minWidth`, which is the one value that can silently change when a style attribute becomes a rule.

## 2. A non-vacuous HTTP header check, now that Environment reports zero
This is the sharper of the two. `environment.html` is currently the **only** page the report-only policy produces anything for, so its two style violations are also what keeps "at least one page reports" from being vacuous. Taking it to zero removes that signal, and a suite that reads a zero-report page as proof of coverage would be proving the opposite of what it says — the failure mode this branch has already hit three times in other forms.
So the header assertion has to carry it:
- the header is present and **byte-identical to the policy the index meta carries**, checked against the response for every trusted path, not inferred from what a page happens to report;
- the negative side keeps its teeth: `/apps/*`, assets and `/api` must have **no** header, so "present on the right paths" is a positive observation rather than a default;
- and the comparison is proved non-vacuous the way the rest of this branch is: a deliberate mismatch must fail it. A test that only ever compares a constant to itself would pass with the policy deleted.

## Unchanged
The meta question is still the one open decision, and its argument is now the guidance alone — see the probe note above, where I withdrew my own claim that the change widens a measured flash. Edits stay held until coord approves.

